### Removed
- Deprecated features
```

## Visualization Guidelines

### Choropleth Maps
- Show HDI or component values across regions
- Intuitive color scales (red-yellow-green for HDI)
- Interactive tooltips with key information
- Zoom and pan capabilities
- Click to select regions

### Time Series
- Clear temporal trends
- Multiple regions comparable
- Year range selection
- Smooth, readable lines

### Component Breakdown
- Clear comparison of three indices
- Bottleneck clearly highlighted
- Bar charts or radar charts
- Consistent color coding

### Color Schemes
- HDI Overall: Red-Yellow-Green gradient
- Health: Red/Orange tones
- Education: Blue tones
- Income: Green tones
- Gender comparisons: Use distinguishable but professional colors

## Streamlit Best Practices

### Performance
- Use `@st.cache_data` for data loading
- Use `@st.cache_resource` for expensive computations
- Load data once, filter dynamically
- Avoid unnecessary reruns

### Layout
- Use `st.set_page_config(layout="wide")` for full width
- Sidebar for controls and filters
- Main area for visualizations
- Clear sections with headers
- Responsive to different screen sizes

### Interactivity
- Sliders for year ranges
- Selectboxes for categorical filters
- Radio buttons for view selection
- Checkboxes for toggles
- All interactions should feel immediate

## Development Workflow

### Initial Setup
1. Create project structure
2. Set up virtual environment
3. Install dependencies
4. Create configuration files
5. Document everything in README

### Feature Development
1. Start with simplest working version
2. Test with actual data
3. Refine based on results
4. Document in CHANGELOG
5. Update README if needed

### Quality Checks
- Code should run without errors
- All features should work as intended
- Documentation should be accurate and complete
- No debug prints or commented-out code in final version
- Linting passes without warnings

## Error Handling

### Data Issues
- Handle missing values gracefully
- Validate data formats
- Provide clear error messages
- Log issues for debugging

### User Input
- Validate all user selections
- Provide sensible defaults
- Prevent invalid combinations
- Clear feedback for issues

## Deployment Considerations

### Requirements
- Pin all dependency versions
- Document Python version requirement
- Include setup instructions
- Provide troubleshooting tips

### Configuration
- Use YAML files for settings
- Environment-specific configs if needed
- Never hardcode paths
- Make customization easy

## What NOT to Do

### Anti-Patterns
- No placeholder comments
- No "TODO: implement this" in production code
- No decorative elements that don't serve function
- No overly complex abstractions
- No premature optimization
- No features "just in case"

### Code Smells to Avoid
- Duplicate code across modules
- Functions longer than 50 lines
- More than 3 levels of nesting
- Unclear variable names
- Magic numbers without explanation

## Communication

### Code Comments
- Explain complex logic
- Document assumptions
- Note data sources
- Clarify non-obvious decisions
- Never state the obvious

### Commit Messages
- Clear, descriptive messages
- Present tense ("Add feature" not "Added feature")
- Reference issues if applicable
- Group related changes

## Testing

### When to Test
- Critical calculations (HDI components, bottlenecks)
- Data processing functions
- Utility functions
- Edge cases

### Test Structure
- Use pytest framework
- One test file per module
- Clear test function names
- Test both success and failure cases

## Future-Proofing

### Extensibility
- Modular code design
- Clear interfaces between modules
- Configuration-driven where possible
- Easy to add new visualizations
- Simple to incorporate new data

### Maintainability
- Self-documenting code
- Up-to-date documentation
- Clear project structure
- Version controlled
- Reproducible environment

## Human Touch

All generated code should appear human-written:
- Natural variable names
- Realistic comments
- Practical error handling
- Logical flow
- No AI-style verbosity or over-documentation
- Professional but not robotic

## Project Success Criteria

A successful implementation will:
1. Load and process subnational HDI data correctly
2. Identify bottlenecking components accurately
3. Visualize data clearly and intuitively
4. Run without errors
5. Be easy to use and understand
6. Be well-documented
7. Look professional and modern
8. Be maintainable and extensible
9. Follow all guidelines in this file

## Final Notes

Remember: The goal is to create a functional, beautiful tool that helps users understand subnational HDI patterns and bottlenecks. Every line of code, every visualization, every piece of documentation should serve this goal. When in doubt, choose simplicity and clarity over complexity and cleverness.

